// `std::shared` — `Shared<T>`, the shared mutable cell behind a value-copied
// handle. Rule 1 copies every binding, argument, field initialization and
// return (spec §6.1), so a struct passed around by value cannot carry mutable
// state in a plain field; a cell is what the language leaves. This is the one
// place reference identity and shared mutation are explicit in the type, so
// the cost is legible at the use site.
//
// WHICH TIER — `Shared` or `Arena` + `Handle`. Reach for `Shared` when
// ownership is genuinely DIFFUSE: several independent holders of the one
// mutable value, no single place that decides when it dies (a signal's value
// and its subscriber list, an rpc mirror's lease counts, a module-level
// registry). Reach for an `Arena` + `Handle` when what you need is a GRAPH or
// a STABLE IDENTITY: nodes that point at each other, entities a wire message
// names, storage whose deletion must leave outstanding references answering
// `None` rather than a ghost. The two are one object at two granularities
// (`destruction.md` §10: a `Shared` is a one-slot counted arena, `clone` is
// the retain and `Weak` is the handle) — the tier follows what the code needs
// to SAY, not which is cheaper.
//
// This headnote used to read "prefer an `Arena` + `Handle` whenever an owner
// exists". The C14 census measured that advice against the estate and found
// 0 of std's 154 `Shared` sites and 0 of kolt's 13 following it: no owner owns
// a cell (only four types are `Disposable`), and what every one of those sites
// wants is interior mutability behind a value-copied handle, which is not a
// slab (`proposal/signal-cell-representation.md` §2.4 finding 1, D8).
//
// NOT for state a closure merely writes. A closure captures the BINDING, not
// the value (spec §6.9), so a `mut` local that closures in the same frame
// assign and read needs no cell at all — M71 retired eight std cells that were
// spelling exactly that the long way.
//
// COUNTING. `clone()` is a retain and a handle's death is a release in the
// design (`destruction.md` §10); today nothing counts, because the JS backend's
// host collector reclaims the box and a cycle through it is the collector's
// problem. The real count arrives with the native backend (C14 S4); what lands
// before it is `Weak<T>` (C1), the edge that makes the graph acyclic.
//
// `Shared` is `external` (an opaque cell): unlike an ordinary aggregate it is
// *not* deep-copied, so `clone()` and ordinary binding share the one underlying
// cell rather than snapshotting it. `read()`/`write()` both reach the cell's
// slot, and their SIGNATURES say what happens there: `read(self): T` hands back
// a value, so storing one copies (spec §6.1), and so does a temporary standing
// at a `&mut self` receiver (`cell.read().push(x)` mutates the copy and is
// lost) — a temporary that only reads the slot, `for x in cell.read()` or
// `cell.read().len()`, copies nothing; `write(self): &mut T
// borrows self` hands back a view, and a write through it is visible through
// every other handle to the same cell.

export *;

external struct Shared<T>;

impl Shared<type T> {
	/// Wrap `value` in a fresh shared cell.
	external fun new(value: T): Shared<T>;

	/// Another handle to the *same* cell (shared, not copied).
	external fun clone(self): Shared<T>;

	/// The cell's value, for reading. A value return (§6.1): binding one,
	/// assigning it, storing it in an aggregate, handing it to an `own`
	/// parameter or standing it at a `&mut self` receiver takes a COPY, so the
	/// cell cannot be grown through it — `read().push(x)` mutates the copy and
	/// is lost, and `write().push(x)` is the spelling that grows the cell. A
	/// temporary that only reads the slot copies nothing.
	external fun read(self): T;

	/// A mutable view of the cell's value, for in-place mutation: `a.write() = x`
	/// rebinds the cell's slot, `a.write().field = …` mutates in place — both
	/// visible through every handle to the cell. `borrows self`: the view projects
	/// the cell, so it cannot outlive this handle.
	external fun write(self): &mut T borrows self;

	/// This CELL's identity: a number that is the same for every handle to one
	/// cell and different for every other cell, for as long as the program
	/// runs. `Shared` is the one place reference identity exists in the
	/// language, so it is the one place that can answer this.
	///
	/// It is not derived from the value — two cells holding equal values are
	/// two cells — and it is not a field: the number is STAMPED on the cell the
	/// first time anything asks, so a program that never asks pays nothing at
	/// all (M66; every `SignalCell` used to mint one at construction for the
	/// rpc runtime's dedup, whether or not the cell was ever exposed).
	///
	/// Stable within one process and meaningless outside it: it is a local
	/// answer to "is this the same cell", never a wire value and never a key
	/// anything persists.
	external fun identity(self): i32;
}
